// =====================================================================
//  bricks-texture.js - the brick image for example 7, stored as text
// =====================================================================
//
// This file holds ONE thing: the brick picture (bricks.png, scaled to
// 512 x 512 and saved as a JPEG), written out as a "data URL". A data
// URL is a whole image file encoded as letters and digits (base64), so
// it can live inside a JavaScript string.
//
// WHY NOT JUST LOAD bricks.png?
// When you open a page by double-clicking it (a file:// address), the
// browser treats every file on disk as coming from a different "origin".
// For security, WebGL refuses to upload an image from another origin
// into a texture: gl.texImage2D throws a SecurityError. An image built
// from a data URL counts as same-origin, so it works on file:// too.
// (On a real web server, loading bricks.png directly works fine.)
//
// 7-texture.html loads this file BEFORE 7-texture.js, so the constant
// below already exists when the main program runs. You don't need to
// read the long string.

const BRICKS_DATA_URL = "data:image/jpeg;base64,";
